#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541266 :question/problem "Find $(u(t)f(t))^{′}$ where $f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 - 3 t^{3} \\\\ 1 - 3 t^{2} \\\\ t - 2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $u(t)=e^{t}.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the scalar function multiple rule, we can write\n\n$$\n(u(t)f(t))^{′}=u^{′}(t)f(t)+u(t)f^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nLet's find $u^{′}(t)$ and $f^{′}(t).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f^{′}(t)&=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2 - 3 t^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1 - 3 t^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t - 2)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}- 9 t^{2} \\\\ - 6 t \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\\\u^{′}(t)&=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(e^{t})=e^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(u(t)f(t))^{′}&=u^{′}(t)f(t)+u(t)f^{′}(t)\\\\&=(e^{t})\\begin{bmatrix}2 - 3 t^{3} \\\\ 1 - 3 t^{2} \\\\ t - 2\\end{bmatrix}+(e^{t})\\begin{bmatrix}- 9 t^{2} \\\\ - 6 t \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\\\&=\\begin{bmatrix}\\left(2 - 3 t^{3}\\right) e^{t} \\\\ \\left(1 - 3 t^{2}\\right) e^{t} \\\\ \\left(t - 2\\right) e^{t}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}- 9 t^{2} e^{t} \\\\ - 6 t e^{t} \\\\ e^{t}\\end{bmatrix}\\\\&=\\begin{bmatrix}\\left(- 3 t^{3} - 9 t^{2} + 2\\right) e^{t} \\\\ \\left(- 3 t^{2} - 6 t + 1\\right) e^{t} \\\\ \\left(t - 1\\right) e^{t}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541268 :question/problem "Find $(u(t)f(t))^{′}$ where $f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}- 3 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} \\\\ e^{t} \\\\ \\frac{1}{t}\\end{bmatrix}$ and $u(t)=t^{2} - 3 t + 2.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the scalar function multiple rule, we can write\n\n$$\n(u(t)f(t))^{′}=u^{′}(t)f(t)+u(t)f^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nLet's find $u^{′}(t)$ and $f^{′}(t).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f^{′}(t)&=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(- 3 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(e^{t}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}- \\frac{3}{t} \\\\ e^{t} \\\\ - \\frac{1}{t^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\\\u^{′}(t)&=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t^{2} - 3 t + 2)=2 t - 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(u(t)f(t))^{′}&=u^{′}(t)f(t)+u(t)f^{′}(t)\\\\&=(2 t - 3)\\begin{bmatrix}- 3 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} \\\\ e^{t} \\\\ \\frac{1}{t}\\end{bmatrix}+(t^{2} - 3 t + 2)\\begin{bmatrix}- \\frac{3}{t} \\\\ e^{t} \\\\ - \\frac{1}{t^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\\\&=\\begin{bmatrix}- 3 \\left(2 t - 3\\right) \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} \\\\ \\left(2 t - 3\\right) e^{t} \\\\ \\frac{2 t - 3}{t}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}- \\frac{3 \\left(t^{2} - 3 t + 2\\right)}{t} \\\\ \\left(t^{2} - 3 t + 2\\right) e^{t} \\\\ - \\frac{t^{2} - 3 t + 2}{t^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\\\&=\\begin{bmatrix}- 6 t \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} - 3 t + 9 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} + 9 - \\frac{6}{t} \\\\ \\left(t^{2} - t - 1\\right) e^{t} \\\\ 1 - \\frac{2}{t^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541281 :question/problem "Use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=2 x + y,\\quad v=y + 2 z,\\quad w=3 z\n$$\n\nto evaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n\\iiint_R \\left(\\left(2 x + y\\right) \\left(y + 2 z\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}V,\n$$\n\n \nwhere the region $R$ is given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z):0\\le 2 x + y\\le 2,\\quad 0\\le y + 2 z\\le 2,\\quad 0\\le z\\le 2\\}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=2 x + y,\\quad v=y + 2 z,\\quad w=3 z\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v,w):0\\le u\\le 2,\\quad 0\\le v\\le 2,\\quad 0\\le w\\le 6\\}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial z} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial z} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(3\\right)(-1)^{3+3}\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(3\\right)\\left(\\left(2\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right)-\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(0\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(x,y,z)}{\\partial(u,v,w)}=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)}\\right)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iiint_R \\left(\\left(2 x + y\\right) \\left(y + 2 z\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}V & =\\iiint_\\Delta \\left(u v\\right)\\cdot \\left|\\frac{1}{6}\\right|\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\mathrm{d}v\\mathrm{d}w \\\\  & =\\iiint_\\Delta \\left(\\frac{u v}{6}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\mathrm{d}v\\mathrm{d}w \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{2}\\int_{0}^{2}\\int_{0}^{6}\\left(\\frac{u v}{6}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}w\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{2}\\int_{0}^{2}\\left(\\left[\\frac{u v w}{6}\\right]_{w=0}^{w=6}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{2}\\int_{0}^{2}\\left(\\left(\\frac{u v \\left(6\\right)}{6}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{u v \\left(0\\right)}{6}\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{2}\\int_{0}^{2}\\left(\\left(u v\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{2}\\int_{0}^{2}\\left(u v\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{2}\\left(\\left[\\frac{u v^{2}}{2}\\right]_{v=0}^{v=2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{2}\\left(\\left(\\frac{u \\left(2\\right)^{2}}{2}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{u \\left(0\\right)^{2}}{2}\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{2}\\left(\\left(2 u\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{2}\\left(2 u\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\left[u^{2}\\right]_{u=0}^{u=2} \\\\  & =\\left(\\left(2\\right)^{2}\\right)-\\left(\\left(0\\right)^{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(4\\right)-\\left(0\\right) \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541290 :question/problem "Use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=- 3 x - y,\\quad v=y + 2 z,\\quad w=3 z\n$$\n\nto evaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n\\iiint_R \\left(\\left(- 3 x - y\\right) \\left(y + 2 z\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}V,\n$$\n\n \nwhere the region $R$ is given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z):0\\le - 3 x - y\\le 3,\\quad 0\\le y + 2 z\\le 1,\\quad 0\\le z\\le 1\\}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=- 3 x - y,\\quad v=y + 2 z,\\quad w=3 z\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v,w):0\\le u\\le 3,\\quad 0\\le v\\le 1,\\quad 0\\le w\\le 3\\}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial z} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial z} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(3\\right)(-1)^{3+3}\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(3\\right)\\left(\\left(-3\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right)-\\left(-1\\right)\\cdot \\left(0\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(x,y,z)}{\\partial(u,v,w)}=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)}\\right)^{-1}=- \\frac{1}{9}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iiint_R \\left(\\left(- 3 x - y\\right) \\left(y + 2 z\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}V & =\\iiint_\\Delta \\left(u v\\right)\\cdot \\left|- \\frac{1}{9}\\right|\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\mathrm{d}v\\mathrm{d}w \\\\  & =\\iiint_\\Delta \\left(\\frac{u v}{9}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\mathrm{d}v\\mathrm{d}w \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{3}\\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{0}^{3}\\left(\\frac{u v}{9}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}w\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{3}\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(\\left[\\frac{u v w}{9}\\right]_{w=0}^{w=3}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{3}\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(\\left(\\frac{u v \\left(3\\right)}{9}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{u v \\left(0\\right)}{9}\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{3}\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(\\left(\\frac{u v}{3}\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{3}\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(\\frac{u v}{3}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{3}\\left(\\left[\\frac{u v^{2}}{6}\\right]_{v=0}^{v=1}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{3}\\left(\\left(\\frac{u \\left(1\\right)^{2}}{6}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{u \\left(0\\right)^{2}}{6}\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{3}\\left(\\left(\\frac{u}{6}\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{3}\\left(\\frac{u}{6}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\left[\\frac{u^{2}}{12}\\right]_{u=0}^{u=3} \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{\\left(3\\right)^{2}}{12}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{\\left(0\\right)^{2}}{12}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)-\\left(0\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541298 :question/problem "Use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=- 3 x - y,\\quad v=y + 2 z,\\quad w=3 z\n$$\n\nto evaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n\\iiint_R \\left(\\left(- 3 x - y\\right) \\left(y + 2 z\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}V,\n$$\n\n \nwhere the region $R$ is given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z):0\\le - 3 x - y\\le 3,\\quad 0\\le y + 2 z\\le 1,\\quad 0\\le z\\le 2\\}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=- 3 x - y,\\quad v=y + 2 z,\\quad w=3 z\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v,w):0\\le u\\le 3,\\quad 0\\le v\\le 1,\\quad 0\\le w\\le 6\\}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial z} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial z} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(3\\right)(-1)^{3+3}\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(3\\right)\\left(\\left(-3\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right)-\\left(-1\\right)\\cdot \\left(0\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(x,y,z)}{\\partial(u,v,w)}=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)}\\right)^{-1}=- \\frac{1}{9}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iiint_R \\left(\\left(- 3 x - y\\right) \\left(y + 2 z\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}V & =\\iiint_\\Delta \\left(u v\\right)\\cdot \\left|- \\frac{1}{9}\\right|\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\mathrm{d}v\\mathrm{d}w \\\\  & =\\iiint_\\Delta \\left(\\frac{u v}{9}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\mathrm{d}v\\mathrm{d}w \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{3}\\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{0}^{6}\\left(\\frac{u v}{9}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}w\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{3}\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(\\left[\\frac{u v w}{9}\\right]_{w=0}^{w=6}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{3}\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(\\left(\\frac{u v \\left(6\\right)}{9}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{u v \\left(0\\right)}{9}\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{3}\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(\\left(\\frac{2 u v}{3}\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{3}\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(\\frac{2 u v}{3}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{3}\\left(\\left[\\frac{u v^{2}}{3}\\right]_{v=0}^{v=1}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{3}\\left(\\left(\\frac{u \\left(1\\right)^{2}}{3}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{u \\left(0\\right)^{2}}{3}\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{3}\\left(\\left(\\frac{u}{3}\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{3}\\left(\\frac{u}{3}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\left[\\frac{u^{2}}{6}\\right]_{u=0}^{u=3} \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{\\left(3\\right)^{2}}{6}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{\\left(0\\right)^{2}}{6}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)-\\left(0\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541305 :question/problem "Use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=3 x + 2 y,\\quad v=y + 2 z,\\quad w=2 z\n$$\n\nto evaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n\\iiint_R \\left(\\left(3 x + 2 y\\right)^{2} \\left(y + 2 z\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}V,\n$$\n\n \nwhere the region $R$ is given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z):1\\le 3 x + 2 y\\le 3,\\quad 1\\le y + 2 z\\le 2,\\quad 1\\le z\\le 3\\}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=3 x + 2 y,\\quad v=y + 2 z,\\quad w=2 z\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v,w):1\\le u\\le 3,\\quad 1\\le v\\le 2,\\quad 2\\le w\\le 6\\}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial z} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial z} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(2\\right)(-1)^{3+3}\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(2\\right)\\left(\\left(3\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right)-\\left(2\\right)\\cdot \\left(0\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(x,y,z)}{\\partial(u,v,w)}=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)}\\right)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iiint_R \\left(\\left(3 x + 2 y\\right)^{2} \\left(y + 2 z\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}V & =\\iiint_\\Delta \\left(u^{2} v\\right)\\cdot \\left|\\frac{1}{6}\\right|\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\mathrm{d}v\\mathrm{d}w \\\\  & =\\iiint_\\Delta \\left(\\frac{u^{2} v}{6}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\mathrm{d}v\\mathrm{d}w \\\\  & =\\int_{1}^{3}\\int_{1}^{2}\\int_{2}^{6}\\left(\\frac{u^{2} v}{6}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}w\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{1}^{3}\\int_{1}^{2}\\left(\\left[\\frac{u^{2} v w}{6}\\right]_{w=2}^{w=6}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{1}^{3}\\int_{1}^{2}\\left(\\left(\\frac{u^{2} v \\left(6\\right)}{6}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{u^{2} v \\left(2\\right)}{6}\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{1}^{3}\\int_{1}^{2}\\left(\\left(u^{2} v\\right)-\\left(\\frac{u^{2} v}{3}\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{1}^{3}\\int_{1}^{2}\\left(\\frac{2 u^{2} v}{3}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{1}^{3}\\left(\\left[\\frac{u^{2} v^{2}}{3}\\right]_{v=1}^{v=2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{1}^{3}\\left(\\left(\\frac{u^{2} \\left(2\\right)^{2}}{3}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{u^{2} \\left(1\\right)^{2}}{3}\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{1}^{3}\\left(\\left(\\frac{4 u^{2}}{3}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{u^{2}}{3}\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{1}^{3}\\left(u^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\left[\\frac{u^{3}}{3}\\right]_{u=1}^{u=3} \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{\\left(3\\right)^{3}}{3}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{\\left(1\\right)^{3}}{3}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(9\\right)-\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{26}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541321 :question/problem "Use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x^{2} + 3 y,\\quad v=x + 2 z,\\quad w=3 z\n$$\n\nto evaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n\\iiint_R \\left(\\left(x + 2 z\\right) \\cos{\\left(x^{2} + 3 y \\right)}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}V,\n$$\n\n \nwhere the region $R$ is given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z):\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\le x^{2} + 3 y\\le \\frac{\\pi}{2},\\quad 1\\le x + 2 z\\le 4,\\quad 1\\le z\\le 3\\}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x^{2} + 3 y,\\quad v=x + 2 z,\\quad w=3 z\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v,w):\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\le u\\le \\frac{\\pi}{2},\\quad 1\\le v\\le 4,\\quad 3\\le w\\le 9\\}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial z} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial z} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 x & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(3\\right)(-1)^{3+3}\\begin{vmatrix}2 x & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(3\\right)\\left(\\left(2 x\\right)\\cdot \\left(0\\right)-\\left(3\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(x,y,z)}{\\partial(u,v,w)}=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)}\\right)^{-1}=- \\frac{1}{9}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iiint_R \\left(\\left(x + 2 z\\right) \\cos{\\left(x^{2} + 3 y \\right)}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}V & =\\iiint_\\Delta \\left(v \\cos{\\left(u \\right)}\\right)\\cdot \\left|- \\frac{1}{9}\\right|\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\mathrm{d}v\\mathrm{d}w \\\\  & =\\iiint_\\Delta \\left(\\frac{v \\cos{\\left(u \\right)}}{9}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\mathrm{d}v\\mathrm{d}w \\\\  & =\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{1}^{4}\\int_{3}^{9}\\left(\\frac{v \\cos{\\left(u \\right)}}{9}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}w\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{1}^{4}\\left(\\left[\\frac{v w \\cos{\\left(u \\right)}}{9}\\right]_{w=3}^{w=9}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{1}^{4}\\left(\\left(\\frac{v \\left(9\\right) \\cos{\\left(u \\right)}}{9}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{v \\left(3\\right) \\cos{\\left(u \\right)}}{9}\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{1}^{4}\\left(\\left(v \\cos{\\left(u \\right)}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{v \\cos{\\left(u \\right)}}{3}\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{1}^{4}\\left(\\frac{2 v \\cos{\\left(u \\right)}}{3}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left(\\left[\\frac{v^{2} \\cos{\\left(u \\right)}}{3}\\right]_{v=1}^{v=4}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left(\\left(\\frac{\\left(4\\right)^{2} \\cos{\\left(u \\right)}}{3}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{\\left(1\\right)^{2} \\cos{\\left(u \\right)}}{3}\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left(\\left(\\frac{16 \\cos{\\left(u \\right)}}{3}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{\\cos{\\left(u \\right)}}{3}\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left(5 \\cos{\\left(u \\right)}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\left[5 \\sin{\\left(u \\right)}\\right]_{u=\\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{u=\\frac{\\pi}{2}} \\\\  & =\\left(5 \\sin{\\left(\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right) \\right)}\\right)-\\left(5 \\sin{\\left(\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right) \\right)}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(5\\right)-\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541328 :question/problem "Use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=3 x - 2 y,\\quad v=y + 2 z,\\quad w=2 z\n$$\n\nto evaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n\\iiint_R \\left(\\left(3 x - 2 y\\right) \\left(y + 2 z\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}V,\n$$\n\n \nwhere the region $R$ is given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z):0\\le 3 x - 2 y\\le 1,\\quad 0\\le y + 2 z\\le 3,\\quad 0\\le z\\le 2\\}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=3 x - 2 y,\\quad v=y + 2 z,\\quad w=2 z\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v,w):0\\le u\\le 1,\\quad 0\\le v\\le 3,\\quad 0\\le w\\le 4\\}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial z} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial z} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(2\\right)(-1)^{3+3}\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(2\\right)\\left(\\left(3\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right)-\\left(-2\\right)\\cdot \\left(0\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(x,y,z)}{\\partial(u,v,w)}=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)}\\right)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iiint_R \\left(\\left(3 x - 2 y\\right) \\left(y + 2 z\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}V & =\\iiint_\\Delta \\left(u v\\right)\\cdot \\left|\\frac{1}{6}\\right|\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\mathrm{d}v\\mathrm{d}w \\\\  & =\\iiint_\\Delta \\left(\\frac{u v}{6}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\mathrm{d}v\\mathrm{d}w \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{0}^{3}\\int_{0}^{4}\\left(\\frac{u v}{6}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}w\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{0}^{3}\\left(\\left[\\frac{u v w}{6}\\right]_{w=0}^{w=4}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{0}^{3}\\left(\\left(\\frac{u v \\left(4\\right)}{6}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{u v \\left(0\\right)}{6}\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{0}^{3}\\left(\\left(\\frac{2 u v}{3}\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{0}^{3}\\left(\\frac{2 u v}{3}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(\\left[\\frac{u v^{2}}{3}\\right]_{v=0}^{v=3}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(\\left(\\frac{u \\left(3\\right)^{2}}{3}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{u \\left(0\\right)^{2}}{3}\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(\\left(3 u\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(3 u\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\left[\\frac{3 u^{2}}{2}\\right]_{u=0}^{u=1} \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{3 \\left(1\\right)^{2}}{2}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{3 \\left(0\\right)^{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)-\\left(0\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541332 :question/problem "Use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=- 3 x + 2 y,\\quad v=y - 2 z,\\quad w=2 z\n$$\n\nto evaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n\\iiint_R \\left(\\left(- 3 x + 2 y\\right) \\left(y - 2 z\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}V,\n$$\n\n \nwhere the region $R$ is given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z):0\\le - 3 x + 2 y\\le 1,\\quad 0\\le y - 2 z\\le 1,\\quad 0\\le z\\le 1\\}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=- 3 x + 2 y,\\quad v=y - 2 z,\\quad w=2 z\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v,w):0\\le u\\le 1,\\quad 0\\le v\\le 1,\\quad 0\\le w\\le 2\\}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial z} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial z} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(2\\right)(-1)^{3+3}\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(2\\right)\\left(\\left(-3\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right)-\\left(2\\right)\\cdot \\left(0\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(x,y,z)}{\\partial(u,v,w)}=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)}\\right)^{-1}=- \\frac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iiint_R \\left(\\left(- 3 x + 2 y\\right) \\left(y - 2 z\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}V & =\\iiint_\\Delta \\left(u v\\right)\\cdot \\left|- \\frac{1}{6}\\right|\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\mathrm{d}v\\mathrm{d}w \\\\  & =\\iiint_\\Delta \\left(\\frac{u v}{6}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\mathrm{d}v\\mathrm{d}w \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{0}^{2}\\left(\\frac{u v}{6}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}w\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(\\left[\\frac{u v w}{6}\\right]_{w=0}^{w=2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(\\left(\\frac{u v \\left(2\\right)}{6}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{u v \\left(0\\right)}{6}\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(\\left(\\frac{u v}{3}\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(\\frac{u v}{3}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(\\left[\\frac{u v^{2}}{6}\\right]_{v=0}^{v=1}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(\\left(\\frac{u \\left(1\\right)^{2}}{6}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{u \\left(0\\right)^{2}}{6}\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(\\left(\\frac{u}{6}\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(\\frac{u}{6}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\left[\\frac{u^{2}}{12}\\right]_{u=0}^{u=1} \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{\\left(1\\right)^{2}}{12}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{\\left(0\\right)^{2}}{12}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{1}{12}\\right)-\\left(0\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541334 :question/problem "Use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x^{2} + 4 y,\\quad v=x + 2 z,\\quad w=3 z\n$$\n\nto evaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n\\iiint_R \\left(\\left(x^{2} + 4 y\\right) \\cos{\\left(x + 2 z \\right)}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}V,\n$$\n\n \nwhere the region $R$ is given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z):1\\le x^{2} + 4 y\\le 3,\\quad \\frac{\\pi}{6}\\le x + 2 z\\le 2 \\pi,\\quad 1\\le z\\le 3\\}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x^{2} + 4 y,\\quad v=x + 2 z,\\quad w=3 z\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v,w):1\\le u\\le 3,\\quad \\frac{\\pi}{6}\\le v\\le 2 \\pi,\\quad 3\\le w\\le 9\\}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial z} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial z} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 x & 4 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(3\\right)(-1)^{3+3}\\begin{vmatrix}2 x & 4 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(3\\right)\\left(\\left(2 x\\right)\\cdot \\left(0\\right)-\\left(4\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(x,y,z)}{\\partial(u,v,w)}=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)}\\right)^{-1}=- \\frac{1}{12}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iiint_R \\left(\\left(x^{2} + 4 y\\right) \\cos{\\left(x + 2 z \\right)}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}V & =\\iiint_\\Delta \\left(u \\cos{\\left(v \\right)}\\right)\\cdot \\left|- \\frac{1}{12}\\right|\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\mathrm{d}v\\mathrm{d}w \\\\  & =\\iiint_\\Delta \\left(\\frac{u \\cos{\\left(v \\right)}}{12}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\mathrm{d}v\\mathrm{d}w \\\\  & =\\int_{1}^{3}\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{3}^{9}\\left(\\frac{u \\cos{\\left(v \\right)}}{12}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}w\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{1}^{3}\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{2 \\pi}\\left(\\left[\\frac{u w \\cos{\\left(v \\right)}}{12}\\right]_{w=3}^{w=9}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{1}^{3}\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{2 \\pi}\\left(\\left(\\frac{u \\left(9\\right) \\cos{\\left(v \\right)}}{12}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{u \\left(3\\right) \\cos{\\left(v \\right)}}{12}\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{1}^{3}\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{2 \\pi}\\left(\\left(\\frac{3 u \\cos{\\left(v \\right)}}{4}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{u \\cos{\\left(v \\right)}}{4}\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{1}^{3}\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{2 \\pi}\\left(\\frac{u \\cos{\\left(v \\right)}}{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{1}^{3}\\left(\\left[\\frac{u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)}}{2}\\right]_{v=\\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{v=2 \\pi}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{1}^{3}\\left(\\left(\\frac{u \\sin{\\left(\\left(2 \\pi\\right) \\right)}}{2}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{u \\sin{\\left(\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right) \\right)}}{2}\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{1}^{3}\\left(\\left(0\\right)-\\left(\\frac{u}{4}\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{1}^{3}\\left(- \\frac{u}{4}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\left[- \\frac{u^{2}}{8}\\right]_{u=1}^{u=3} \\\\  & =\\left(- \\frac{\\left(3\\right)^{2}}{8}\\right)-\\left(- \\frac{\\left(1\\right)^{2}}{8}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(- \\frac{9}{8}\\right)-\\left(- \\frac{1}{8}\\right) \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541338 :question/problem "Use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x^{2} + 4 y,\\quad v=x + 2 z,\\quad w=3 z\n$$\n\nto evaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n\\iiint_R \\left(\\left(x + 2 z\\right) \\cos{\\left(x^{2} + 4 y \\right)}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}V,\n$$\n\n \nwhere the region $R$ is given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z):\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\le x^{2} + 4 y\\le \\frac{\\pi}{2},\\quad 1\\le x + 2 z\\le 2,\\quad 1\\le z\\le 3\\}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x^{2} + 4 y,\\quad v=x + 2 z,\\quad w=3 z\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v,w):\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\le u\\le \\frac{\\pi}{2},\\quad 1\\le v\\le 2,\\quad 3\\le w\\le 9\\}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial z} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial z} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 x & 4 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(3\\right)(-1)^{3+3}\\begin{vmatrix}2 x & 4 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(3\\right)\\left(\\left(2 x\\right)\\cdot \\left(0\\right)-\\left(4\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(x,y,z)}{\\partial(u,v,w)}=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)}\\right)^{-1}=- \\frac{1}{12}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iiint_R \\left(\\left(x + 2 z\\right) \\cos{\\left(x^{2} + 4 y \\right)}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}V & =\\iiint_\\Delta \\left(v \\cos{\\left(u \\right)}\\right)\\cdot \\left|- \\frac{1}{12}\\right|\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\mathrm{d}v\\mathrm{d}w \\\\  & =\\iiint_\\Delta \\left(\\frac{v \\cos{\\left(u \\right)}}{12}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\mathrm{d}v\\mathrm{d}w \\\\  & =\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{1}^{2}\\int_{3}^{9}\\left(\\frac{v \\cos{\\left(u \\right)}}{12}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}w\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{1}^{2}\\left(\\left[\\frac{v w \\cos{\\left(u \\right)}}{12}\\right]_{w=3}^{w=9}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{1}^{2}\\left(\\left(\\frac{v \\left(9\\right) \\cos{\\left(u \\right)}}{12}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{v \\left(3\\right) \\cos{\\left(u \\right)}}{12}\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{1}^{2}\\left(\\left(\\frac{3 v \\cos{\\left(u \\right)}}{4}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{v \\cos{\\left(u \\right)}}{4}\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{1}^{2}\\left(\\frac{v \\cos{\\left(u \\right)}}{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left(\\left[\\frac{v^{2} \\cos{\\left(u \\right)}}{4}\\right]_{v=1}^{v=2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left(\\left(\\frac{\\left(2\\right)^{2} \\cos{\\left(u \\right)}}{4}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{\\left(1\\right)^{2} \\cos{\\left(u \\right)}}{4}\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left(\\left(\\cos{\\left(u \\right)}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{\\cos{\\left(u \\right)}}{4}\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left(\\frac{3 \\cos{\\left(u \\right)}}{4}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\left[\\frac{3 \\sin{\\left(u \\right)}}{4}\\right]_{u=\\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{u=\\frac{\\pi}{2}} \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{3 \\sin{\\left(\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right) \\right)}}{4}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{3 \\sin{\\left(\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right) \\right)}}{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{3}{8}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541340 :question/problem "Use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x - \\frac{y}{4},\\quad v=\\frac{y}{4},\\quad w=\\frac{z}{2}\n$$\n\nto evaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n\\iiint_R \\left(x - \\frac{y}{4} + \\frac{z}{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}V,\n$$\n\n \nwhere the region $R$ is given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z):1\\le x - \\frac{y}{4}\\le 3,\\quad 1\\le \\frac{y}{4}\\le 3,\\quad 1\\le z\\le 2\\}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x - \\frac{y}{4},\\quad v=\\frac{y}{4},\\quad w=\\frac{z}{2}\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v,w):1\\le u\\le 3,\\quad 1\\le v\\le 3,\\quad \\frac{1}{2}\\le w\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial z} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial z} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & - \\frac{1}{4} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{4} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)(-1)^{3+3}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & - \\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{4}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\left(\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)-\\left(- \\frac{1}{4}\\right)\\cdot \\left(0\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(x,y,z)}{\\partial(u,v,w)}=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)}\\right)^{-1}=8.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iiint_R \\left(x - \\frac{y}{4} + \\frac{z}{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}V & =\\iiint_\\Delta \\left(u + w\\right)\\cdot \\left|8\\right|\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\mathrm{d}v\\mathrm{d}w \\\\  & =\\iiint_\\Delta \\left(8 u + 8 w\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\mathrm{d}v\\mathrm{d}w \\\\  & =\\int_{1}^{3}\\int_{1}^{3}\\int_{\\frac{1}{2}}^{1}\\left(8 u + 8 w\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}w\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{1}^{3}\\int_{1}^{3}\\left(\\left[8 u w + 4 w^{2}\\right]_{w=\\frac{1}{2}}^{w=1}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{1}^{3}\\int_{1}^{3}\\left(\\left(4 \\left(1\\right)^{2}\\right)-\\left(4 \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^{2}\\right)+\\left(8 u \\left(1\\right)\\right)-\\left(8 u \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{1}^{3}\\int_{1}^{3}\\left(\\left(4\\right)-\\left(1\\right)+\\left(8 u\\right)-\\left(4 u\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{1}^{3}\\int_{1}^{3}\\left(4 u + 3\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{1}^{3}\\left(\\left[v \\left(4 u + 3\\right)\\right]_{v=1}^{v=3}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{1}^{3}\\left(\\left(3 \\left(3\\right)\\right)-\\left(3 \\left(1\\right)\\right)+\\left(4 u \\left(3\\right)\\right)-\\left(4 u \\left(1\\right)\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{1}^{3}\\left(\\left(9\\right)-\\left(3\\right)+\\left(12 u\\right)-\\left(4 u\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{1}^{3}\\left(8 u + 6\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\left[4 u^{2} + 6 u\\right]_{u=1}^{u=3} \\\\  & =\\left(4 \\left(3\\right)^{2}\\right)-\\left(4 \\left(1\\right)^{2}\\right)+\\left(6 \\left(3\\right)\\right)-\\left(6 \\left(1\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(36\\right)-\\left(4\\right)+\\left(18\\right)-\\left(6\\right) \\\\  & =44\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541346 :question/problem "Which of the following shows the region in the $uv$-plane where the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}4 u - 12 \\\\ 2 \\left(v + 2\\right)^{2}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nis (locally) orientation-reversing?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing at the points where the Jacobian determinant is negative:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}<0\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}4 u - 12 \\\\ 2 \\left(v + 2\\right)^{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} &=\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 v + 8\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=(4)\\cdot (4 v + 8)-(0)\\cdot (0) \\\\ &=16 v + 32.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the required regions, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16 v + 32 &<0 \\\\ v < -2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the equation\n\n$$\nv = -2\n$$\n\ndefines a horizontal line.\n\nTherefore, the region where the transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing must be the following:\n\n![](images/0d/0d6d817325324661cf7b0b3a73a89a8d63558bd90ae268c7598092d5a1354c49.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541347 :question/problem "Which of the following shows the region in the $uv$-plane where the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}2 v + \\left(u - 3\\right)^{2} + 4 \\\\ - 3 u - \\left(v + 2\\right)^{2} + 9\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nis (locally) orientation-reversing?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing at the points where the Jacobian determinant is negative:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}<0\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}2 v + \\left(u - 3\\right)^{2} + 4 \\\\ - 3 u - \\left(v + 2\\right)^{2} + 9\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} &=\\begin{vmatrix}2 u - 6 & 2 \\\\ -3 & - 2 v - 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=(2 u - 6)\\cdot (- 2 v - 4)-(2)\\cdot (-3) \\\\ &=- 4 u v - 8 u + 12 v + 30.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the required regions, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}- 4 u v - 8 u + 12 v + 30 &<0 \\\\ \\left(u - 3\\right) \\left(v + 2\\right) > \\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the solution $v > -2 + \\frac{3}{2 \\left(u - 3\\right)}$ for $u>3$ and $v < -2 + \\frac{3}{2 \\left(u - 3\\right)}$ for $u<3.$ At $u=3,$ the Jacobian is positive, so this line is excluded from the required region.\n\nNotice that the equation\n\n$$\nv = -2 + \\frac{3}{2 \\left(u - 3\\right)}\n$$\n\ndefines a hyperbola.\n\nTherefore, the region where the transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing must be the following:\n\n![](images/dd/ddaffc1b9c44fc6aa22caac176f1fdd6e171c31e1246d884d25101473977fa9c.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541352 :question/problem "Which of the following shows the region in the $uv$-plane where the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}u + 3 \\\\ \\left(v + 1\\right)^{2}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nis (locally) orientation-preserving?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a transformation is (locally) orientation-preserving at the points where the Jacobian determinant is positive:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}>0\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}u + 3 \\\\ \\left(v + 1\\right)^{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} &=\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 v + 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=(1)\\cdot (2 v + 2)-(0)\\cdot (0) \\\\ &=2 v + 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the required regions, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 v + 2 &>0 \\\\ v > -1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the equation\n\n$$\nv = -1\n$$\n\ndefines a horizontal line.\n\nTherefore, the region where the transformation is (locally) orientation-preserving must be the following:\n\n![](images/6b/6bc5057212ab1c347102f5c09aa4d21eb9bb0f6215ed7294be4b30c127e90deb.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541357 :question/problem "Which of the following shows the region in the $uv$-plane where the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}v + \\left(u + 3\\right)^{2} + 1 \\\\ - 3 u - \\left(v + 1\\right)^{2} - 9\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nis (locally) orientation-preserving?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a transformation is (locally) orientation-preserving at the points where the Jacobian determinant is positive:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}>0\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}v + \\left(u + 3\\right)^{2} + 1 \\\\ - 3 u - \\left(v + 1\\right)^{2} - 9\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} &=\\begin{vmatrix}2 u + 6 & 1 \\\\ -3 & - 2 v - 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=(2 u + 6)\\cdot (- 2 v - 2)-(1)\\cdot (-3) \\\\ &=- 4 u v - 4 u - 12 v - 9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the required regions, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}- 4 u v - 4 u - 12 v - 9 &>0 \\\\ \\left(u + 3\\right) \\left(v + 1\\right) < \\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the solution $v < -1 + \\frac{3}{4 \\left(u + 3\\right)}$ for $u>-3$ and $v > -1 + \\frac{3}{4 \\left(u + 3\\right)}$ for $u<-3.$ At $u=-3,$ the Jacobian is positive, so this line belongs to the required region.\n\nNotice that the equation\n\n$$\nv = -1 + \\frac{3}{4 \\left(u + 3\\right)}\n$$\n\ndefines a hyperbola.\n\nTherefore, the region where the transformation is (locally) orientation-preserving must be the following:\n\n![](images/a7/a77c173f49ff007692a1f040708adecbe65b7b035ce776faef93de1f8ce6019a.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541361 :question/problem "Which of the following shows the region in the $uv$-plane where the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\left(u + 3\\right)^{2} \\\\ 2 v + 2\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nis (locally) orientation-reversing?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing at the points where the Jacobian determinant is negative:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}<0\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\left(u + 3\\right)^{2} \\\\ 2 v + 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} &=\\begin{vmatrix}8 u + 24 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=(8 u + 24)\\cdot (2)-(0)\\cdot (0) \\\\ &=16 u + 48.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the required regions, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16 u + 48 &<0 \\\\ u < -3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the equation\n\n$$\nu = -3\n$$\n\ndefines a vertical line.\n\nTherefore, the region where the transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing must be the following:\n\n![](images/c5/c55116b6c9a7a78b548eaef6002dc56584ddf8498adb550335b3bf27593fb7ea.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541364 :question/problem "Which of the following shows the region in the $uv$-plane where the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}2 v + \\left(u + 1\\right)^{2} + 2 \\\\ - u - \\left(v + 1\\right)^{2} - 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nis (locally) orientation-preserving?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a transformation is (locally) orientation-preserving at the points where the Jacobian determinant is positive:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}>0\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}2 v + \\left(u + 1\\right)^{2} + 2 \\\\ - u - \\left(v + 1\\right)^{2} - 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} &=\\begin{vmatrix}2 u + 2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & - 2 v - 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=(2 u + 2)\\cdot (- 2 v - 2)-(2)\\cdot (-1) \\\\ &=- 4 u v - 4 u - 4 v - 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the required regions, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}- 4 u v - 4 u - 4 v - 2 &>0 \\\\ \\left(u + 1\\right) \\left(v + 1\\right) < \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the solution $v < -1 + \\frac{1}{2 \\left(u + 1\\right)}$ for $u>-1$ and $v > -1 + \\frac{1}{2 \\left(u + 1\\right)}$ for $u<-1.$ At $u=-1,$ the Jacobian is positive, so this line belongs to the required region.\n\nNotice that the equation\n\n$$\nv = -1 + \\frac{1}{2 \\left(u + 1\\right)}\n$$\n\ndefines a hyperbola.\n\nTherefore, the region where the transformation is (locally) orientation-preserving must be the following:\n\n![](images/61/61eb7087a9aa310143a93f0dd0cf549196c66536baa0570bb3b7158b934e38ff.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541368 :question/problem "Which of the following shows the region in the $uv$-plane where the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}4 u + 12 \\\\ 4 \\left(v - 3\\right)^{2}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nis (locally) orientation-preserving?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a transformation is (locally) orientation-preserving at the points where the Jacobian determinant is positive:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}>0\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}4 u + 12 \\\\ 4 \\left(v - 3\\right)^{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} &=\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8 v - 24\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=(4)\\cdot (8 v - 24)-(0)\\cdot (0) \\\\ &=32 v - 96.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the required regions, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}32 v - 96 &>0 \\\\ v > 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the equation\n\n$$\nv = 3\n$$\n\ndefines a horizontal line.\n\nTherefore, the region where the transformation is (locally) orientation-preserving must be the following:\n\n![](images/64/645c29d8beb5ddfe4c108002292a7fc13c6fc62dba4a6f616d2e4cbce3181278.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541390 :question/problem "Which of the following shows the region in the $uv$-plane where the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}3 v + \\left(u + 3\\right)^{2} - 9 \\\\ - u - \\left(v - 3\\right)^{2} - 3\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nis (locally) orientation-preserving?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a transformation is (locally) orientation-preserving at the points where the Jacobian determinant is positive:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}>0\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}3 v + \\left(u + 3\\right)^{2} - 9 \\\\ - u - \\left(v - 3\\right)^{2} - 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} &=\\begin{vmatrix}2 u + 6 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 6 - 2 v\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=(2 u + 6)\\cdot (6 - 2 v)-(3)\\cdot (-1) \\\\ &=- 4 u v + 12 u - 12 v + 39.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the required regions, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}- 4 u v + 12 u - 12 v + 39 &>0 \\\\ \\left(u + 3\\right) \\left(v - 3\\right) < \\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the solution $v < 3 + \\frac{3}{4 \\left(u + 3\\right)}$ for $u>-3$ and $v > 3 + \\frac{3}{4 \\left(u + 3\\right)}$ for $u<-3.$ At $u=-3,$ the Jacobian is positive, so this line belongs to the required region.\n\nNotice that the equation\n\n$$\nv = 3 + \\frac{3}{4 \\left(u + 3\\right)}\n$$\n\ndefines a hyperbola.\n\nTherefore, the region where the transformation is (locally) orientation-preserving must be the following:\n\n![](images/53/5389d4bb3047ce09fdd363b74f11656401529f5385409f693c18a1c20cac51ec.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541393 :question/problem "Which of the following shows the region in the $uv$-plane where the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\left(u + 1\\right)^{2} \\\\ 3 v + 3\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nis (locally) orientation-preserving?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a transformation is (locally) orientation-preserving at the points where the Jacobian determinant is positive:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}>0\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\left(u + 1\\right)^{2} \\\\ 3 v + 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} &=\\begin{vmatrix}8 u + 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=(8 u + 8)\\cdot (3)-(0)\\cdot (0) \\\\ &=24 u + 24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the required regions, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}24 u + 24 &>0 \\\\ u > -1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the equation\n\n$$\nu = -1\n$$\n\ndefines a vertical line.\n\nTherefore, the region where the transformation is (locally) orientation-preserving must be the following:\n\n![](images/b9/b99527183109e4b5f9e870ddd4f0e2738f2642b22038990272263926155e7256.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541395 :question/problem "Which of the following shows the region in the $uv$-plane where the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}v + \\left(u + 3\\right)^{2} + 1 \\\\ - 3 u - \\left(v + 1\\right)^{2} - 9\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nis (locally) orientation-reversing?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing at the points where the Jacobian determinant is negative:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}<0\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}v + \\left(u + 3\\right)^{2} + 1 \\\\ - 3 u - \\left(v + 1\\right)^{2} - 9\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} &=\\begin{vmatrix}2 u + 6 & 1 \\\\ -3 & - 2 v - 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=(2 u + 6)\\cdot (- 2 v - 2)-(1)\\cdot (-3) \\\\ &=- 4 u v - 4 u - 12 v - 9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the required regions, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}- 4 u v - 4 u - 12 v - 9 &<0 \\\\ \\left(u + 3\\right) \\left(v + 1\\right) > \\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the solution $v > -1 + \\frac{3}{4 \\left(u + 3\\right)}$ for $u>-3$ and $v < -1 + \\frac{3}{4 \\left(u + 3\\right)}$ for $u<-3.$ At $u=-3,$ the Jacobian is positive, so this line is excluded from the required region.\n\nNotice that the equation\n\n$$\nv = -1 + \\frac{3}{4 \\left(u + 3\\right)}\n$$\n\ndefines a hyperbola.\n\nTherefore, the region where the transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing must be the following:\n\n![](images/a1/a14192698baa5f54314d4325bcbc3e9d488ec728b0beee1570921e0869259da3.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542130 :question/problem "The slope of the tangent to the curve $y=\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}+2x}$ at the point where $x=1$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nWe use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=-{x}^{2}+2x.$ Then $y=\\sqrt{u}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-2x+2.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot (-2x+2) \\\\  & =\\frac{-2x+2}{2\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}+2x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=1} & ={\\left(\\frac{-2x+2}{2\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}+2x}}\\right)|}_{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2(1)+2}{2\\sqrt{-{1}^{2}+2(1)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{2\\sqrt{1}} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542221 :question/problem "Find the slope of the tangent to the curve $y=\\sqrt{x-3}+3{x}^{3}$ at the point where $x=4.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nLet $f(x)=\\sqrt{x-3}.$ We use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=x-3.$ Then $f=\\sqrt{u}={u}^{1/2}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{-1/2}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x-3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the sum rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x-3}}+9{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=4,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=4} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{4-3}}+9(4)^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+144 \\\\  & =\\frac{289}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542222 :question/problem "The inequality $0<x\\le 7$ can be expressed in interval notation as" :question/worked-solution "Every inequality can be expressed as an interval. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses $()$ if the endpoints are *not* included.\n\n- Brackets $[]$ if the endpoints *are* included.\n\nFor the given inequality \n\n$$\n0\\,\\,<\\,\\,x\\,\\,\\le \\,\\,7\n$$\n\nwe note the following:\n\n- The left endpoint $0$ is *not* included in the interval. Therefore, we use $(\\,.$\n\n- The right endpoint $7$ *is* included in the interval. Therefore, we use $]\\,.$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding interval is\n\n$$\nx\\in (0,7].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542282 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=2x+y,$ traversed along the line segment from $(-1,4)$ to $(- \\frac{5}{2},2)?$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(-1,4)$ to $(- \\frac{5}{2},2).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=-i+4\\,j$ and $b=-\\frac{5}{2}\\,i+2\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(-i+4\\,j)+t[(-\\frac{5}{2}\\,i+2\\,j)-(-i+4\\,j)] \\\\  & =(-i+4\\,j)+t(-\\frac{3}{2}\\,i-2\\,j) \\\\  & =(-\\frac{3}{2}t-1)\\,i+(4-2t)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=-\\frac{3}{2}t-1$ and $y=4-2t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\frac{3}{2}t-1)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4-2t)j \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\,i-2\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\left(- \\frac{3}{2}\\right)^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=2x+y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =2(-\\frac{3}{2}t-1)+(4-2t) \\\\  & =-5t+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-5t+2)\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-5t+2)\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542291 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=1+x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,$ traversed along the line segment from $(-1,2)$ to $(\\frac{3}{2},0)?$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(-1,2)$ to $(\\frac{3}{2},0).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=-i+2\\,j$ and $b=\\frac{3}{2}\\,i.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(-i+2\\,j)+t[\\frac{3}{2}\\,i-(-i+2\\,j)] \\\\  & =(-i+2\\,j)+t(\\frac{5}{2}\\,i-2\\,j) \\\\  & =(\\frac{5}{2}t-1)\\,i+(-2t+2)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=\\frac{5}{2}t-1$ and $y=-2t+2$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{5}{2}t-1)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-2t+2)j \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\,i-2\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\frac{5}{2}}^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{41}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=1+x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =1+(\\frac{5}{2}t-1)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-2t+2) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{5}{2}t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-2))\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{41}}{2}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{41}}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{5}{2}t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-2))\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542298 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-y,$ traversed along the line segment from $(3,- \\frac{7}{2})$ to $(-1,- \\frac{11}{3})?$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(3,- \\frac{7}{2})$ to $(-1,- \\frac{11}{3}).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=3\\,i-\\frac{7}{2}\\,j$ and $b=-i-\\frac{11}{3}\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(3\\,i-\\frac{7}{2}\\,j)+t[(-i-\\frac{11}{3}\\,j)-(3\\,i-\\frac{7}{2}\\,j)] \\\\  & =(3\\,i-\\frac{7}{2}\\,j)+t(-4\\,i-\\frac{1}{6}\\,j) \\\\  & =(-4t+3)\\,i+(-\\frac{7}{2}-\\frac{1}{6}t)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=-4t+3$ and $y=-\\frac{7}{2}-\\frac{1}{6}t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-4t+3)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\frac{7}{2}-\\frac{1}{6}t)j \\\\  & =-4\\,i-\\frac{1}{6}\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-4)^{2}+\\left(- \\frac{1}{6}\\right)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{577}}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(-4t+3)^{2}-(-\\frac{7}{2}-\\frac{1}{6}t) \\\\  & =16{t}^{2}-\\frac{143}{6}t+\\frac{25}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(16{t}^{2}-\\frac{143}{6}t+\\frac{25}{2})\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{577}}{6}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{577}}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(16{t}^{2}-\\frac{143}{6}t+\\frac{25}{2})\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542305 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=2x+y,$ traversed along the line segment from $(-1,\\frac{13}{3})$ to $(- \\frac{5}{2},2)?$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(-1,\\frac{13}{3})$ to $(- \\frac{5}{2},2).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=-i+\\frac{13}{3}\\,j$ and $b=-\\frac{5}{2}\\,i+2\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(-i+\\frac{13}{3}\\,j)+t[(-\\frac{5}{2}\\,i+2\\,j)-(-i+\\frac{13}{3}\\,j)] \\\\  & =(-i+\\frac{13}{3}\\,j)+t(-\\frac{3}{2}\\,i-\\frac{7}{3}\\,j) \\\\  & =(-\\frac{3}{2}t-1)\\,i+(\\frac{13}{3}-\\frac{7}{3}t)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=-\\frac{3}{2}t-1$ and $y=\\frac{13}{3}-\\frac{7}{3}t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\frac{3}{2}t-1)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{13}{3}-\\frac{7}{3}t)j \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\,i-\\frac{7}{3}\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\left(- \\frac{3}{2}\\right)^{2}+\\left(- \\frac{7}{3}\\right)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{277}}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=2x+y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =2(-\\frac{3}{2}t-1)+(\\frac{13}{3}-\\frac{7}{3}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{16}{3}t+\\frac{7}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-\\frac{16}{3}t+\\frac{7}{3})\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{277}}{6}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{277}}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-\\frac{16}{3}t+\\frac{7}{3})\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542320 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y),$ traversed along the line segment from $(1,3)$ to $(\\frac{7}{2},\\frac{13}{3})?$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(1,3)$ to $(\\frac{7}{2},\\frac{13}{3}).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=i+3\\,j$ and $b=\\frac{7}{2}\\,i+\\frac{13}{3}\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(i+3\\,j)+t[(\\frac{7}{2}\\,i+\\frac{13}{3}\\,j)-(i+3\\,j)] \\\\  & =(i+3\\,j)+t(\\frac{5}{2}\\,i+\\frac{4}{3}\\,j) \\\\  & =(1+\\frac{5}{2}t)\\,i+(3+\\frac{4}{3}t)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=1+\\frac{5}{2}t$ and $y=3+\\frac{4}{3}t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+\\frac{5}{2}t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3+\\frac{4}{3}t)j \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\,i+\\frac{4}{3}\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\frac{5}{2}}^{2}+{\\frac{4}{3}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{17}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((1+\\frac{5}{2}t)+3+\\frac{4}{3}t) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4+\\frac{23}{6}t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4+\\frac{23}{6}t)\\cdot \\frac{17}{6}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{17}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4+\\frac{23}{6}t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542325 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-y,$ traversed along the line segment from $(-2,- \\frac{7}{2})$ to $(1,-1)?$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(-2,- \\frac{7}{2})$ to $(1,-1).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=-2\\,i-\\frac{7}{2}\\,j$ and $b=i-j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(-2\\,i-\\frac{7}{2}\\,j)+t[(i-j)-(-2\\,i-\\frac{7}{2}\\,j)] \\\\  & =(-2\\,i-\\frac{7}{2}\\,j)+t(3\\,i+\\frac{5}{2}\\,j) \\\\  & =(3t-2)\\,i+(-\\frac{7}{2}+\\frac{5}{2}t)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=3t-2$ and $y=-\\frac{7}{2}+\\frac{5}{2}t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t-2)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\frac{7}{2}+\\frac{5}{2}t)j \\\\  & =3\\,i+\\frac{5}{2}\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{\\frac{5}{2}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{61}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(3t-2)^{2}-(-\\frac{7}{2}+\\frac{5}{2}t) \\\\  & =9{t}^{2}-\\frac{29}{2}t+\\frac{15}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(9{t}^{2}-\\frac{29}{2}t+\\frac{15}{2})\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{61}}{2}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{61}}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(9{t}^{2}-\\frac{29}{2}t+\\frac{15}{2})\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542327 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y),$ traversed along the line segment from $(1,3)$ to $(\\frac{7}{2},4)?$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(1,3)$ to $(\\frac{7}{2},4).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=i+3\\,j$ and $b=\\frac{7}{2}\\,i+4\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(i+3\\,j)+t[(\\frac{7}{2}\\,i+4\\,j)-(i+3\\,j)] \\\\  & =(i+3\\,j)+t(\\frac{5}{2}\\,i+j) \\\\  & =(1+\\frac{5}{2}t)\\,i+(3+t)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=1+\\frac{5}{2}t$ and $y=3+t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+\\frac{5}{2}t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3+t)j \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\frac{5}{2}}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{29}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((1+\\frac{5}{2}t)+3+t) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4+\\frac{7}{2}t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4+\\frac{7}{2}t)\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{29}}{2}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{29}}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4+\\frac{7}{2}t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542330 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=1+x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,$ traversed along the line segment from $(-1,2)$ to $(\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{1}{3})?$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(-1,2)$ to $(\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{1}{3}).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=-i+2\\,j$ and $b=\\frac{3}{2}\\,i+\\frac{1}{3}\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(-i+2\\,j)+t[(\\frac{3}{2}\\,i+\\frac{1}{3}\\,j)-(-i+2\\,j)] \\\\  & =(-i+2\\,j)+t(\\frac{5}{2}\\,i-\\frac{5}{3}\\,j) \\\\  & =(\\frac{5}{2}t-1)\\,i+(-\\frac{5}{3}t+2)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=\\frac{5}{2}t-1$ and $y=-\\frac{5}{3}t+2$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{5}{2}t-1)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\frac{5}{3}t+2)j \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\,i-\\frac{5}{3}\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\frac{5}{2}}^{2}+\\left(- \\frac{5}{3}\\right)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\sqrt{13}}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=1+x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =1+(\\frac{5}{2}t-1)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\frac{5}{3}t+2) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5}{3}t-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{5}{2}t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5}{3}t-2))\\cdot \\frac{5\\sqrt{13}}{6}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\sqrt{13}}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{5}{2}t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5}{3}t-2))\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542335 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral of the function $f(x,y)={e}^{x+y},$ traversed along the line segment from $(0,0)$ to $(\\frac{3}{2},1)?$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(0,0)$ to $(\\frac{3}{2},1).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=0$ and $b=\\frac{3}{2}\\,i+j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =0+t\\,(b-0) \\\\  & =t(b) \\\\  & =t(\\frac{3}{2}\\,i+j) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}t\\,i+t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=\\frac{3}{2}t$ and $y=t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{3}{2}t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\frac{3}{2}}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{13}{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={e}^{x+y}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & ={e}^{\\frac{3}{2}t+t} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\frac{5}{2}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{\\frac{5}{2}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{2}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{\\frac{5}{2}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542336 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral of the function $f(x,y)={e}^{x+y},$ traversed along the line segment from $(0,0)$ to $(\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{4}{3})?$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(0,0)$ to $(\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{4}{3}).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=0$ and $b=\\frac{3}{2}\\,i+\\frac{4}{3}\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =0+t\\,(b-0) \\\\  & =t(b) \\\\  & =t(\\frac{3}{2}\\,i+\\frac{4}{3}\\,j) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}t\\,i+\\frac{4}{3}t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=\\frac{3}{2}t$ and $y=\\frac{4}{3}t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{3}{2}t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{4}{3}t)j \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\,i+\\frac{4}{3}\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\frac{3}{2}}^{2}+{\\frac{4}{3}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{145}{36}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{145}}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={e}^{x+y}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & ={e}^{\\frac{3}{2}t+\\frac{4}{3}t} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\frac{17}{6}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{\\frac{17}{6}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{145}}{6}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{145}}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{\\frac{17}{6}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542375 :question/problem "Evaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n\\iiint_R \\sqrt{(x)^{2}+(y)^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}V\n$$\n\nover the region $R$ enclosed inside the cylinder $x^2+y^2=1$ and between the surfaces $z=0$ and $z=3.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iiint_R f(x,y,z)\\,dV=\\iiint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=r\\sin\\theta,\\quad z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/c7/c7fe69ab2583c066df73bbf6719e4c58fea1ed565e345bc62c11b7ebc779508b.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $1$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=0=0,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=3=3.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta,z):0\\le r\\le 1,\\ 0\\le\\theta<2\\cdot \\pi,\\ 0\\le z\\le 3\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iiint_R \\sqrt{(x)^{2}+(y)^{2}}\\,dV &=\\iiint_\\Delta (\\sqrt{(r\\cdot \\cos(\\theta))^2+(r\\cdot \\sin(\\theta))^2})\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}\\int_0^{1}\\int_{0}^{3}(r)^{2}\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}\\int_0^{1}(r)^{2}[z]_{z=0}^{z=3}\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}\\int_0^{1}(r)^{2}((3)-(0))\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}\\int_0^{1}3\\cdot (r)^{2}\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}[(r)^{3}]_{r=0}^{r=1}\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}(((1))^{3})-(((0))^{3})\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}(1)-(0)\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}1\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=[\\theta]_{\\theta=0}^{\\theta=2\\cdot \\pi} \\\\ &=(2\\cdot \\pi)-(0) \\\\ &=2\\cdot \\pi\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542378 :question/problem "Evaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n\\iiint_R \\sqrt{(x)^{2}+(y)^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}V\n$$\n\nover the region $R$ enclosed inside the cylinder $x^2+y^2=1$ and between the surfaces $z=0$ and $z=9-2\\cdot y.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iiint_R f(x,y,z)\\,dV=\\iiint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=r\\sin\\theta,\\quad z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/c7/c7fe69ab2583c066df73bbf6719e4c58fea1ed565e345bc62c11b7ebc779508b.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $1$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=0=0,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=9-2\\cdot y=-2\\cdot r\\cdot \\sin(\\theta)+9.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta,z):0\\le r\\le 1,\\ 0\\le\\theta<2\\cdot \\pi,\\ 0\\le z\\le -2\\cdot r\\cdot \\sin(\\theta)+9\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iiint_R \\sqrt{(x)^{2}+(y)^{2}}\\,dV &=\\iiint_\\Delta (\\sqrt{(r\\cdot \\cos(\\theta))^2+(r\\cdot \\sin(\\theta))^2})\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}\\int_0^{1}\\int_{0}^{-2\\cdot r\\cdot \\sin(\\theta)+9}(r)^{2}\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}\\int_0^{1}(r)^{2}[z]_{z=0}^{z=-2\\cdot r\\cdot \\sin(\\theta)+9}\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}\\int_0^{1}(r)^{2}((-2\\cdot r\\cdot \\sin(\\theta)+9)-(0))\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}\\int_0^{1}-2\\cdot (r)^{3}\\cdot \\sin(\\theta)+9\\cdot (r)^{2}\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}[-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (r)^{4}\\cdot \\sin(\\theta)+3\\cdot (r)^{3}]_{r=0}^{r=1}\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}(-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot ((1))^{4}\\cdot \\sin(\\theta))-(-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot ((0))^{4}\\cdot \\sin(\\theta))+(3\\cdot ((1))^{3})-(3\\cdot ((0))^{3})\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}(-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\sin(\\theta))-(0)+(3)-(0)\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}3-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\sin(\\theta)\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=[3\\cdot \\theta+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\cos(\\theta)]_{\\theta=0}^{\\theta=2\\cdot \\pi} \\\\ &=(6\\cdot \\pi)-(0)+\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\ &=6\\cdot \\pi\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542381 :question/problem "Evaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n\\iiint_R \\sqrt{(x)^{2}+(y)^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}V\n$$\n\nover the region $R$ enclosed inside the cylinder $x^2+y^2=16$ and between the surfaces $z=0$ and $z=3.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iiint_R f(x,y,z)\\,dV=\\iiint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=r\\sin\\theta,\\quad z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/ce/ce06d6892ad079a03c395b9ddac5db712d0b0e1797ec63c3c27f005c2287adb5.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $4$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=0=0,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=3=3.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta,z):0\\le r\\le 4,\\ 0\\le\\theta<2\\cdot \\pi,\\ 0\\le z\\le 3\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iiint_R \\sqrt{(x)^{2}+(y)^{2}}\\,dV &=\\iiint_\\Delta (\\sqrt{(r\\cdot \\cos(\\theta))^2+(r\\cdot \\sin(\\theta))^2})\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}\\int_0^{4}\\int_{0}^{3}(r)^{2}\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}\\int_0^{4}(r)^{2}[z]_{z=0}^{z=3}\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}\\int_0^{4}(r)^{2}((3)-(0))\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}\\int_0^{4}3\\cdot (r)^{2}\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}[(r)^{3}]_{r=0}^{r=4}\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}(((4))^{3})-(((0))^{3})\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}(64)-(0)\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}64\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=[64\\cdot \\theta]_{\\theta=0}^{\\theta=2\\cdot \\pi} \\\\ &=(128\\cdot \\pi)-(0) \\\\ &=128\\cdot \\pi\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542388 :question/problem "Evaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n\\iiint_R 2\\cdot \\sqrt{(x)^{2}+(y)^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}V\n$$\n\nover the region $R$ enclosed inside the cylinder $x^2+y^2=9$ and between the surfaces $z=0$ and $z=1.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iiint_R f(x,y,z)\\,dV=\\iiint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=r\\sin\\theta,\\quad z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/8e/8ee9b491452a26902c4800a006c16f1bbca31f0e800ec9aad20dfeef68eed8f4.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $3$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=0=0,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=1=1.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta,z):0\\le r\\le 3,\\ 0\\le\\theta<2\\cdot \\pi,\\ 0\\le z\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iiint_R 2\\cdot \\sqrt{(x)^{2}+(y)^{2}}\\,dV &=\\iiint_\\Delta (2\\cdot \\sqrt{(r\\cdot \\cos(\\theta))^2+(r\\cdot \\sin(\\theta))^2})\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}\\int_0^{3}\\int_{0}^{1}2\\cdot (r)^{2}\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}\\int_0^{3}2\\cdot (r)^{2}[z]_{z=0}^{z=1}\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}\\int_0^{3}2\\cdot (r)^{2}((1)-(0))\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}\\int_0^{3}2\\cdot (r)^{2}\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}[\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot (r)^{3}]_{r=0}^{r=3}\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}(\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot ((3))^{3})-(\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot ((0))^{3})\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}(18)-(0)\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}18\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=[18\\cdot \\theta]_{\\theta=0}^{\\theta=2\\cdot \\pi} \\\\ &=(36\\cdot \\pi)-(0) \\\\ &=36\\cdot \\pi\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542389 :question/problem "Evaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n\\iiint_R 2\\cdot z\\cdot ((x)^{2}+(y)^{2})^{-\\frac{1}{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}V\n$$\n\nover the region $R$ enclosed inside the cylinder $x^2+y^2=9$ and between the surfaces $z=0$ and $z=\\sqrt{-(x)^{2}+(y)^{2}+14}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iiint_R f(x,y,z)\\,dV=\\iiint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=r\\sin\\theta,\\quad z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/8e/8ee9b491452a26902c4800a006c16f1bbca31f0e800ec9aad20dfeef68eed8f4.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $3$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=0=0,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=\\sqrt{-(x)^{2}+(y)^{2}+14}=\\sqrt{-(r)^{2}\\cdot \\cos(2\\cdot \\theta)+14}.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta,z):0\\le r\\le 3,\\ 0\\le\\theta<2\\cdot \\pi,\\ 0\\le z\\le \\sqrt{-(r)^{2}\\cdot \\cos(2\\cdot \\theta)+14}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iiint_R 2\\cdot z\\cdot ((x)^{2}+(y)^{2})^{-\\frac{1}{2}}\\,dV &=\\iiint_\\Delta (2\\cdot \\frac{z}{\\sqrt{(r\\cdot \\cos(\\theta))^2+(r\\cdot \\sin(\\theta))^2}})\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}\\int_0^{3}\\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{-(r)^{2}\\cdot \\cos(2\\cdot \\theta)+14}}2\\cdot z\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}\\int_0^{3}[(z)^{2}]_{z=0}^{z=\\sqrt{-(r)^{2}\\cdot \\cos(2\\cdot \\theta)+14}}\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}\\int_0^{3}(((\\sqrt{-(r)^{2}\\cdot \\cos(2\\cdot \\theta)+14}))^{2})-(((0))^{2})\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}\\int_0^{3}-(r)^{2}\\cdot \\cos(2\\cdot \\theta)+14\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}[-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (r)^{3}\\cdot \\cos(2\\cdot \\theta)+14\\cdot r]_{r=0}^{r=3}\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}(-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot ((3))^{3}\\cdot \\cos(2\\cdot \\theta))-(-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot ((0))^{3}\\cdot \\cos(2\\cdot \\theta))+(14\\cdot (3))-(14\\cdot (0))\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}(-9\\cdot \\cos(2\\cdot \\theta))-(0)+(42)-(0)\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}42-9\\cdot \\cos(2\\cdot \\theta)\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=[42\\cdot \\theta-9\\cdot \\cos(\\theta)\\cdot \\sin(\\theta)]_{\\theta=0}^{\\theta=2\\cdot \\pi} \\\\ &=(84\\cdot \\pi)-(0)+(0)-(0) \\\\ &=84\\cdot \\pi\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
